 "narrative": "All delivered estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive signed values support the registered association and negative signed values oppose it. The one robust new finding is H85: PTEN-loss cells have stronger dependency_STAT3 than PTEN-intact cells. The signed estimate is +0.267 (95% CI +0.215 to +0.319, R73), corresponding to a raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast of about -0.267, i.e. a more negative STAT3 dependency score in PTEN-loss cells. The effect is large relative to the STAT3 score distribution, about one STAT3 standard deviation, and the interval is precise and far from zero. This is not a borderline small effect and is much larger than the null pathway modifiers observed in this batch. Given its magnitude and precision, I accept H85 as a dataset association and request independent validation to test whether it replicates and to guard against multiplicity or hidden batch/co-mutational structure. It should still be interpreted as an association, not causation or a generalized biological claim.\n\nH37 also provides strong new evidence: within ARID1A-intact cells, SMARCA4-loss versus SMARCA4-intact cells have much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, signed estimate +0.777 (95% CI +0.756 to +0.798, R75). This is nearly as large as the already validated main SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association and confirms that the association is not confined to the ARID1A-loss context. I accept and close H37 as a context-stratum result; validation is unnecessary because the main effect has already been independently validated and this direct stratum is highly precise and concordant.\n\nThe remaining new comparisons do not support scientifically meaningful associations. RB1-loss was not associated with KIF18A dependency (H79: +0.008, CI -0.019 to +0.036) or with WRN dependency outside MSI-high status (H86: +0.015, CI -0.022 to +0.052). MYC amplification was not associated with KIF18A dependency (H56: +0.006, CI -0.017 to +0.028). NF1-loss showed no support for stronger KRAS dependency (H80 signed estimate is negative and CI crosses zero: -0.020, CI -0.049 to +0.009), and its suggested weaker BRAF dependency was small and imprecise (H81: +0.021, CI -0.005 to +0.047). ERBB2 amplification was not associated with EGFR dependency (H82: +0.003, CI -0.023 to +0.030), and its possible STAT3 association was small and CI-crossing (H83: +0.022, CI -0.015 to +0.059). PTEN loss did not show the hypothesized weaker EGFR dependency (H84: signed estimate negative, -0.006, CI -0.029 to +0.017). Finally, the cleaner RAS context checks were null: the BRAF-V600E effect on BRAF dependency among KRAS-wild-type cells was small and slightly opposite (H26: -0.019, CI -0.051 to +0.013), and the KRAS-mutant effect on KRAS dependency among BRAF-wild-type cells remained small and imprecise (H58: +0.014, CI -0.004 to +0.032). I reject and close these unsupported claims. The new PTEN/STAT3 context checks are registered to assess whether the effect is stable by lineage subtype and whether it is modified by PIK3CA mutation, but the immediate priority is independent validation of the main H85 association.",
 "research_notes": "Core accepted/validated dataset associations remain: MSI-high -> stronger dependency_WRN; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency_SMARCA2; TP53-intact -> stronger dependency_USP7; KEAP1 loss -> weaker/less-negative dependency_NFE2L2. H38 ARID1A-loss SMARCA4/SMARCA2 context is validated; H37 now also confirms the same effect in ARID1A-intact cells and is closed. New robust dataset discovery: PTEN loss -> stronger dependency_STAT3 (H85: signed +0.267, 95% CI +0.215 to +0.319, R73), accepted and active with validation requested. All other newly tested predictors were null or too small/imprecise: RB1 loss vs KIF18A/WRN, MYC vs KIF18A, NF1 loss vs KRAS/BRAF, ERBB2 amplification vs EGFR/STAT3, PTEN loss vs EGFR, and cleaner KRAS/BRAF subgroup contrasts. Next priorities: assess H85 validation when it arrives; then examine PTEN/STAT3 context stability by lineage subtype and PIK3CA mutation, and possibly add technical or co-mutational context checks only if validation supports a durable association. Do not spend validation on small CI-crossing pathway modifiers or on accepted effects already validated. Maintain distinction between dataset associations and causal/generalizable claims.",
 "validate": "H85"
}

